    \label{fig:network_traces}
\end{figure}

Remark on Comparative Fairness and Information Asymmetry: A critical reader might argue that providing explicit future MPD metadata (e.g., $\vec{v}_{t:t+M-1}$ and $\vec{c}_{t:t+M-1}$) to our agent grants it an unfair ``oracle'' advantage over baselines. However, this comparison is strictly fair for two reasons. First, control-theoretic baselines like RobustMPC fundamentally require and estimate upcoming chunk sizes over their sliding prediction horizon to solve their internal optimization, meaning they implicitly rely on future trajectories. Second, to definitively isolate the source of our performance gains, we rely on our \textit{Ablation Study (Section V.E)}. We explicitly tested a baseline equivalent to ``Standard PPO + Future Info'' (without Lyapunov regularization). As shown later, simply feeding future knowledge to a standard RL agent causes it to over-explore and fail catastrophically (yielding a 37.3\% rebuffering rate). It is exclusively the structural \textit{combination} of future-awareness and our proposed Lyapunov-inspired safety constraint that unlocks the reported performance, validating that the gain is not a mere artifact of information asymmetry.

